A||||11||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||Interior parts||||1||
Z||000000||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||12-17-2000||05:41 PM||||Where can replacement plastic pieces in the center console (ash tray lids, etc.) be found.  These parts look disgusting in my car, they are white as if they were cancerous tumours.  As Arnold says "it's not a tumour!"<P>Also, are there fabric reupholstery kits out there. I have found only leather.  <P>If you have the saddle (tanish brown) interior parts for a 1984 then email me at djtuttle@wwc.com.  I just bought my car and it runs great, but the interior looks filthy and gross to touch.  <P>Last thing, who would you recommend for a replacement headliner (w/o sunroof)?<P>Ever see "Ferris Beuler's Day Off"?  His sister "Jeanie" had a white Fiero, remember it?  In the movie, it looks like it is brand new.  That's my goal . . . can you help?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||FieroJoe||12-17-2000||05:45 PM||||Welcome to the Forum!<P>Look on ebay for some of the parts, though Ash Tray doors in good condition get pretty steep as they are sometimes really hard to find.<BR>    As for cloth replacement upholstry, the Fiero Store has some, <A HREF="http://www.fierostore.com" TARGET=_blank>www.fierostore.com</A> <BR>    Many of the members on the forum may also have the parts that you are looking for.  Post a topic in "the mall" section with a list of parts that you are looking for.<P>------------------<BR>FieroJoe<BR>c311t@yahoo.com<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Mark||12-17-2000||05:56 PM||||As far as the headliner is concerned, you can have it redone at a local auto trim and upolstery shop.  Another approach is to redo it yourself.  The biggest part of the job is removing the headliner backing board.  Even if you don't replace the material yourself, removing/replacing the headliner board can save you some bucks.  Use the search function...there should be lots of threads with detailed info on headliner replacement.||||reg||1
Z||000003||tgowens||12-17-2000||06:39 PM||||come see us at the Fiero Shop when you have a chance - we have or can get the parts and we can do the headliner as well.......635 S. 31st St., Richmond, CA 94804 - 510-233-4376 -- we specalize in repairs and resorations of the Fiero.......||||reg||1
Z||000004||MrPBody||12-17-2000||08:53 PM||||If you're into it, you can sometimes find these things in a self-service salvage yard.  Grubby fun.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||rogergarrison||12-18-2000||12:54 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by FieroJoe:<BR><B>Welcome to the Forum!<P>Look on ebay for some of the parts, though Ash Tray doors in good condition get pretty steep as they are sometimes really hard to find.<BR>    As for cloth replacement upholstry, the Fiero Store has some, <A HREF="http://www.fierostore.com" TARGET=_blank>www.fierostore.com</A> <BR>    Many of the members on the forum may also have the parts that you are looking for.  Post a topic in "the mall" section with a list of parts that you are looking for.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>DAMN JOE!! why didnt u tell me that, lol, when i redid my kitkar interior i threw away the ashtrays and doors - i dont smoke. they had never even been used when i bought the car. my bigggggggg baddddddd!<P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||DXR_DAD||12-18-2000||06:20 PM||||Lucky Here are a few pics of what I'm doing:<P> <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=99075&a=723593&p=35728572&Sequence=0&res=high"> <P> <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=99075&a=723593&p=35728575&Sequence=0&res=high"> <P> <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=99075&a=723593&p=35728569&Sequence=0&res=high"> <P>Working on an all light gray interior.<P>DXR_DAD||||reg||10
Z||000007||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||12-19-2000||02:54 AM||||DRXDAD, your interior looks great.  Excellent work indeed.  Now I see how far I have to go.  <BR>My car was owned by smokers who let ashes get into the carpets, etc.  There is black smoke residue/tar in all of the plastic and vinyl.  I think I will start with a good cleaning.  Any suggestions on how to get 16 years of filth out of my interior?  What solvents are best?  Can I use Simple Green or will it further damage the interior?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||DXR_DAD||12-19-2000||04:05 AM||||Thanks lucky, I just cleaned a center console<BR>today I used a bath room tile cleaner, that's cause I did it in the bath tub  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> It was close at hand. It did a good job!!!!<P><BR>A good cleaning would be the first step. I'm sure simple green will work fine. <BR>Just take your time and get it the way you want, I've been working on mine for a few months now, and still not finished.<P><BR>DXR_DAD||||reg||14
Z||000009||Monkeyman||12-19-2000||06:42 PM||||The Fiero Store sells replacement '86-'88 GT style cloth seat covers.  Unfortunately they are about the same price as Mr. Mikes leather (and don't look as good, IMHO).||||reg||1
Z||000010||Frank Sombers||12-19-2000||08:11 PM||||Lucky:<BR>The best thing I've ever used to clean  interior vynil/leather/naugahide is Kiwi Saddle Soap. Over the years I used this on MG's, Mustangs, and now my Fiero with great results. <BR>Take a toothbrush (preferrably one you no longer use, or one belonging to your wife or sister) put some saddle soap onto the bristles, dip it into a cup of water and then GENTLY scrub a small area working up a GOOD LATHER. Wipe off with a damp cloth and proceed to another area. The results are like night and day. You don't have to scrub hard, it's more important to work a good lather into the piece your working on. It takes time but believe me the results are worth it. Also, this is not a corrosive cleaner, it adds life to the vynil instead of drying it out. <BR>I did this my recently purchased '88 cpe. which was always owned by non smokers, and looked good. I couldn't believe the difference between where I did and didn't clean. This method takes removes years of ground in grim and dirt. I  usually finish with a coat of Armorall.<BR>Good luck with the cleaning!<BR>Frank   ||||reg||3
Z||000011||Seanh||12-19-2000||11:20 PM||||Good way to clean the carpet is to vacuum it real good. Remove the seats and I used a drop of dish soap w/hot water in a bucket. Applied it with a stiff bush and scrubbed, alot. Then vacuumed it out with my ShopVac and did the same with the seats. Let them dry and reinstalled. The turned out great and it smells almost new. If that doesnt get the smells out, use Fabreez: Gets bad smells out of fabrices, for good.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/transsig.gif">   <BR><A HREF="http://www.kdsi.net/~sean" TARGET=_blank>Sean's Fiero Page</A><BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8 Auto<BR>Going on to 3.4 in Jan.<BR><I>"It's always in the last place you look." Of course it is. Why would you keep looking after you've found it? Do people do this? Who and where are they?</I>||||reg||1
